##### Code Contract Verification in Practice 

I use Visual Studio 2012 to execute the code contracts using Code Contracts. 

1. Launch Visual Studio 2012

2. Select Pex Projects located at Project Collection Level

3. Right-click on the root node and select "Execute Code Contracts"

The Pex Project has a reference to .Net framework 4.5.

4. A report is generated in the Output window providing detailed description of contracts and test cases. 

Some of the error messages are:

```
Assert.True( Old_arrListContainsX != New_arrListContainsX );

The current test framework does not support the 'Assert.True' method. Consider using the 'Debug.Assert' method instead.
```

I believe this is due to the error because some of the contracts that were marked as verified were not being picked up by the test runner. 


# Conclusion 

This paper explored the integration of Pex (Practical Code Ex), a tool developed by Microsoft Research, into a Visual Studio 2012 environment. Pex harnesses automated theorem proving and heuristic search to design a diverse set of test cases, including unit and integration tests for code written in diverse languages. The paper has also highlighted the concept of code contracts, a set of static-checkable properties of a program that can be applied via attributes, enabling automated verification. Lastly, it highlighted some challenges in leveraging Pex with Code Contracts, and provided a potential solution. There is room for future research on this topic by exploring the integration of Pex with Code Contracts. 

