When you run the test, you will get a warning message that you need to remove the observers. This is because the Pex engine does not support the Debug.Assert. However, you can replace the Debug.Assert line with a normal assertion, or remove the whole Debug.Assert line. 

To make these modifications:

1. In Visual Studio, locate the arrayListContractTest.cs file, then open it.

2. Find and replace all remaining Debug.Assert statements starting from line 157 to line 191, replacing them with regular assert statements (for example, `Assert.IsTrue(Old_result < New_result);`).

### Run the test with the code fixer
You can run the test with the code fixer to automatically fix the Pex test that are not passing. Here is the general process:

1. In Visual Studio, navigate to Test > Generate Code Coverage Data... > Run All in Test Explorer. This will execute all your tests in test mode.

2. Navigate to Test > Run All Tests in the Test Explorer. This will run the tests that Pex has generated, which should not have the debug.assert statements. These tests should pass.

3. Run the Pex-Generated code fixer. Right-click on the solution > Code fixes > Run Code Analysis to get Pex-Generated Code Fixes. This will automatically fix the tests to the point where they are no longer throwing debug.assert exceptions.

While the above steps should help you troubleshoot a failing Pex test, it's always a good practice to manually test your code manually and observe its behavior and handle all potential exceptions appropriately for your needs. 

